== Legacy and media ==
Following the assassination of Caesar and the ensuing civil war afterward, multiple novels, plays, and films were created surrounding the plot, the most famous of which was the 1599 play ''[[Julius Caesar (play)|Julius Caesar]]'' by [[William Shakespeare]].

=== Film and television ===

* 1914: [[Julius Caesar (1914 film)|''Julius Caesar'']], an Italian film focusing on Caesar's return to Rome, the Ides of March, and the civil war.
* 1950: [[Julius Caesar (1950 film)|''Julius Caesar'']], starring Charlton Heston
* 1953: ''[[William Shakespeare's Julius Caesar]],'' a film adaptation of Shakespeare's play.
* 1954: "[[Rinse the Blood Off My Toga]]", a comedy sketch by [[Wayne and Shuster]] reimagining both the play and the event as a detective noir story. First performed on radio and later reprised many times on television.
* 1970: [[Julius Caesar (1970 film)|''Julius Caesar'']], another film adaptation of Shakespeare's play.
* 2002: [[Julius Caesar (miniseries)|Julius Caesar]], a miniseries surrounding Caesar's life and assassination.
* 2005: [[Rome (TV series)|Rome]], an HBO TV-series focusing on events from the end of the Gallic wars through the rise of Octavian (52BCE-31BCE).

